==English==
{{wp}}

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*dʰregʰ-}}
From {{inh|en|enm|draught}}, {{m|enm|draght}}, {{m|enm|draȝt}}, from {{inh|en|ang|*dreaht}}, {{m|ang|*dræht}} (related to {{m|ang|dragan|t=to [[draw]], [[drag]]}}), from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*drahtuz}}, noun form of {{m|gem-pro|*draganą}}; equivalent to {{suf|en|draw|t|id2=th}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/dɹɑːft/|a=RP}}
*: {{rhymes|en|ɑːft|s=1}}
* {{IPA|en|/dɹæft/|a=US}}
*: {{rhymes|en|æft|s=1}}
*: {{audio|en|En-us-draught.ogg|a=US}}
* {{audio|en|EN-AU ck1 draught.ogg|a=AU}}
* {{homophones|en|draft}}
* {{q|common in the 16–18th centuries, now nonstandard}} {{IPA|en|/dɹɔt/}}<ref>{{R:OED}}: "in late ME. the word [draught] was sometimes ''drawt'', whence the frequent (drǭt) in 16th-18th c. rimes"</ref><ref>{{R:Collins}}: "A pronunciation (drɔt) is sometimes heard for ''draught'', perhaps because -aught is frequently pronounced (-ɔt) elsewhere, as in ''caught'' and ''taught''."</ref>

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{label|en|British spelling}} {{alt form|en|draft#Noun}} ''in some of its senses''.
#: {{ux|en|She could feel a '''draught''' where she was sitting.}}
#* {{RQ:Tyndale NT|Luke|V|text={{...}}he sayde vnto Simon: Cary vs into the depe, and lett slippe thy nett to make a '''draught'''.}}
#* {{RQ:Melville Moby-Dick|[[s:Moby-Dick (1851) US edition/Chapter 36|chapter 36]]|text=“Drink and pass!” he cried, handing the heavy charged flagon to the nearest seaman. “The crew alone now drink. Round with it, round! Short '''draughts'''—long swallows, men; ’tis hot as Satan’s hoof.}}
#* {{RQ:Maugham Moon and Sixpence|[[s:The Moon and Sixpence/Chapter XXXV|chapter 35]]||Finally I gave him a '''draught''', and he sank into uneasy slumber.}}
#* '''1927-29''', {{w|M.K. Gandhi}}, ''{{w|The Story of My Experiments with Truth}}'', translated '''1940''' by {{w|Mahadev Desai}}, [https://www.mkgandhi.org/autobio/chap3.htm Part I, Chapter iii]:
#*: Much as I wish that I had not to write this chapter, I know that I shall have to swallow many such bitter '''draughts''' in the course of this narrative. And I cannot do otherwise, if I claim to be a worshipper of Truth. {{...}}
# {{lb|en|British}} A [[checker]]: a [[game]] [[piece]] [[use]]d in the [[game]] of [[draughts]].
# {{lb|en|Australia}} [[ale|Ale]]: a [[type]] of [[beer]] [[brew]]ed [[using]] [[top-fermenting]] [[yeast]].
# {{lb|en|UK|medicine|obsolete}} A [[mild]] [[vesicatory]].
# {{lb|en|UK|obsolete}} An [[outhouse]]: an [[outbuilding]] [[use]]d as a [[lavatory]].
#* {{RQ:Tyndale NT|Matthew|XV|text=Then sayde Jesus: are ye yett withoute understondinge? perceave ye not, that whatsoever goeth in at the mouth, descendeth doune into the bely, and ys cast out into the '''draught'''?}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Timon of Athens|passage=Rid me these Villaines from your companies; / Hang them, or stab them, drowne them in a '''draught''', / Confound them by some course, and come to me, / Ile giue you Gold enough.}}
# {{lb|en|UK|obsolete}} [[any|Any]] [[picture]] or [[drawing]].
#* {{RQ:Browne Pseudodoxia Epidemica|edition=2nd|V|22|text=And therefore, for the whole process, and full representation, there must be more than one '''draught'''; the one representing him in station, the other in session, another in genuflexion.}}
# {{lb|en|UK|obsolete}} A [[sudden]] [[attack]] upon an [[enemy]].
#* {{RQ:Spenser Ireland|text=drawing sudden '''draughts''' upon the enemy when he looketh not for you}}

====Synonyms====
* {{sense|outhouse}} {{l|en|draught-house}}; ''see also'' '''[[Thesaurus:outhouse]]'''
* {{sense|game piece}} {{l|en|checker}} (used in [[checkers]])

