===Etymology 1===
{{etymon|en|id=broche|:inh|enm:broche|tree=1}}
From {{inh|en|enm|broche}}, from {{der|en|fro|broche}}, from {{der|en|la-vul|*brocca}}, originally a feminine form of {{der|en|la|broccus}}, perhaps ultimately of {{der|en|cel-gau|-}} origin (see {{cog|gd|bròg}}). {{doublet|en|brochure}}.<ref>{{R:Online Etymology Dictionary}}</ref>

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# A series of [[chisel]] [[point]]s [[mount]]ed on one piece of [[steel]]. For example, the toothed stone chisel shown [[w:Chisel#Stone|here]].
# {{lb|en|masonry}} A broad [[chisel]] for stone-cutting.
# {{alternative spelling of|en|brooch}}.
#* {{RQ:Landon Romance|page=123|volume=III|passage=Mr. S. had a large straw hat, and striped jacket and trousers, and his shirt fastened at the throat by a '''broach''' with Carry's hair, for he was always quite above wearing a neckcloth.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2012|author=Cara C. Putman|title=A Promise Born
|passage=She pinned a '''broach''' on her jacket.<br />When Viv saw it, she laughed. “Is that the best you can do? A flower '''broach'''?”}}
# A [[spit]] for cooking food.
#* {{RQ:Bacon Henry 7
|passage=He turned a '''broach''' that had worn a crown.}}
# An [[awl]]; a [[bodkin]]; also, a wooden rod or pin, sharpened at each end, used by [[thatcher]]s.
# {{lb|en|architecture|UK|dialect}} A [[spire]] rising from a [[tower]].
# A spit-like [[start]] on the head of a young [[stag]].
# The stick from which [[candle]] [[wick]]s are suspended for dipping.<ref>{{R:Knight AMD}}</ref>
# The [[pin]] in a [[lock]] which enters the [[barrel]] of the [[key]].

